<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="improper-ints-complete">
    <title>Improper Integrals (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Solutions to Schrödinger equations are used to determine probabilities. In order to extract useful
                        information, we need to know how to integrate over <q>all of space</q> (for example, in one-dimensional 
                        space, this means integrating over <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>).
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Probability Densities</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         If <m>\psi(x,t) : \R^2 \to \C</m> is a solution to a Schrödinger equation, then
                         <md>
                            |\psi(x,t)|^2 = 
                         </md>
                         is a 
                         
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>|\psi(x,t)|^2 = \overline{\psi} \cdot \psi</m> (which is <em>real</em> -- look back to properties of
                        complex multiplication with conjugates!) is a <term>probability density function</term> (this is the 
                        <term>Born interpretation</term> of wave functions).
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Practically, this means that the probability a particle has position between <m>x=a</m> and <m>x=b</m> at 
                        time <m>t</m> is 
                        <md>
                            \int_a^b |\psi|^2 \quad dx
                        </md>.
                        Since a particle can technically be anywhere in (one-dimensional) space, we must make sense of integrals 
                        of the form
                        <md>
                            \int_{-\infty}^\infty f(x) \quad dx
                        </md>.
                        Integrals with infinite bounds are known as <term>improper integrals</term>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="1in">
                <title>Improper Integrals</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    Define
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \int_c^\infty f(x) \quad dx \amp= </mrow>

                        <mrow> \int_{-\infty}^c f(x) \quad dx \amp= </mrow>

                        <mrow> \int_{-\infty}^\infty f(x) \quad dx \amp= </mrow>
                    </md>
                    
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Here is the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/fkenbkd9gf">Improper Integral Desmos interactive</url>
                        used to illustrate the intuition behind the definitions of improper integrals:
                        <md>
                        <mrow> \int_c^\infty f(x) \quad dx \amp= \lim_{t \to \infty}  \int_c^t f(x)\quad dx </mrow>

                        <mrow> \int_{-\infty}^c f(x) \quad dx \amp= \lim_{t \to -\infty}\int_{t}^c f(x) \quad dx</mrow>

                        <mrow> \int_{-\infty}^\infty f(x) \quad dx \amp= \int_{-\infty}^c f(x) \quad dx + \int_c^\infty f(x) \quad dx </mrow>
                        </md>,
                        assuming both limits exist. In this case, we say the integral <m>\int_{-\infty}^\infty f(x) \quad dx</m> <term>converges</term>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
              
                
            </exercise>
 </page>
        <page>

            <exercise>
                <title>Examples: Improper Integrals</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        We'll compute a couple of examples by hand to add experience to our intuition, and focus on 
                        important applications of improper integrals next time.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task workspace="5in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                         <m> \displaystyle     \int_1^{\infty} \frac{1}{x} \quad dx </m>  
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow> \lim_{t \to \infty} \int_1^t \frac{1}{x} \quad dx \amp= \lim_{t\to \infty} (\ln|t| - \ln(1) ) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \lim_{t \to \infty} \ln(t) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \infty </mrow>
                            </md>.
                            This integral is not convergent (it <term>diverges</term>).
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                
                <task workspace="5in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>
                               \displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{1}{1+x^2} \quad dx
                            </m>
                            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow> \lim_{t \to \infty} \displaystyle \int_{0}^{t} \frac{1}{1+x^2} \quad dx \amp= \lim_{t \to \infty}(\arctan(t)-\arctan(0)) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \lim_{t \to \infty} \arctan(t) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \frac{\pi}{2} </mrow>
                            </md>
                            
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow> \lim_{t \to -\infty} \displaystyle \int_{t}^{0} \frac{1}{1+x^2} \quad dx \amp= \lim_{t \to \infty}(\arctan(0)-\arctan(t)) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \lim_{t \to \infty} -\arctan(t) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \frac{\pi}{2} </mrow>
                            </md>
                            
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            So this integral converges, and 
                            <m>
                               \displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{1}{1+x^2} \quad dx = \frac{\pi}{2} + \frac{\pi}{2} = \pi
                            </m>.
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>
                               \displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2} \quad dx
                            </m>
                            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            For now, just note that 
                             <m>
                               \displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2} \quad dx = \sqrt{\pi}
                            </m>. You can use the Desmos interactive above to verify this is reasonable.
                            We'll see ways to justify this later once we have more tools.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
            </exercise>

    </page>

       
    </worksheet>
